#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paints the three levels of Navigation's painted maps, for the world's zones and for the instances (dungeons and
raids) alike, by one pipeline (Doc/guides/map-style.md; Saneroki, 2026-10-21: strict maps of the layout, not free
concept art). Every subject has one frame, its box in the world, that all three levels share, all three seen
straight from above (Saneroki, 2026-10-22), so they lie over each other exactly and every road, place and pin is in
the same spot in each. Each zone is painted on its own; stitch joins the zones along their outlines.

  close    the terrain, pixel-exact: the Atlas' own squares (dev/tools/atlas: one terrain tile at 2048 pixels, the
           game's minimap textures softened, its water flattened), the Atlas' words (atlas.toml) with the subject's
           colours and light, the Atlas' check against the game's water and lava (up to three seeds) and its
           colour pass. An instance's terrain is its own map's tiles with the indoor maps of the buildings they
           place laid over them, as the game's minimap draws them (dev/tools/indoor-art's parts, placed as Navigation
           places them). The samples paint the squares around the zone's main place, or over the whole instance.
  middle   a map in the manner of the game's own, laid exactly over the terrain, drawn in pencil and ink on
           parchment, straight from above (levels.toml, [middle]): image 1 the game's map art in the frame (a zone's
           full map; an instance floor's map, its title banner painted over; where the game has none, the terrain
           plan, another place's map for its manner only), image 2 the terrain plan, image 3 the parchment: the
           game maps' yellow-orange, a zone's turned towards its land's colour (land_tone: greenish, yellowish,
           reddish, whitish by what it is). A zone in one picture, an instance floor by floor.
  far      a photograph of the land taken straight down from high above, its layout kept with a little leeway
           (levels.toml, [far]), in one picture, painted from noise, the Fun ControlNet Union keeping its layout:
             a zone      image 1 its terrain, its water a sea's blue, lightly blurred and toned down (far_colours:
                         its textures gone, its roads and towns faint); image 2 a piece of the close level; the
                         controls its heights (height_map) and the lines where the walking data's water meets land
                         (Core's set Walking, guide_of); its words name the zones reaching into its frame
             an instance image 1 its plan, blurred and toned down; image 2 a piece of the close level; the
                         control the plan's clearest edges (an instance has no walking data)
           (Not the middle level's drawing: it was copied. The sharp terrain as image 1 came back as the game's
           own; the walking data's barriers as lines came back as lakes.)

Each middle and far painting is checked twice and painted again with the next seed until one passes, TRIES at most:
  outlines  how well its edges agree with the terrain plan's (their normalised correlation at OUTLINE pixels): a
            middle level against how well the game's own map agrees with it (MIDDLE of that), a far one at least
            FAR and at most COPY (more is its image 1 come back as it is);
  water     a zone's far painting: its broad water against the walking data's (atlas' check, a shore straying up to
            FAR_STRAY, FAR_WATER at most added or lost);
  looks     the vision model (qwen3vl-look) answers in JSON: writing in it; a pencil map on tinted parchment seen
            from above (middle); a real place, not a map or panels, seen straight down, of the brief's kind (far).

Usage:
  dev/tools/map-layers inputs [--zones UIMAPS] [--instances MAPS] [--continents DIR --tables DIR --minimap DIR]
                              [--instance-files DIR] [--mapart DIR] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers paint [--levels close,middle,far] [--whole-close] [--only NAMES] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers join [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers stitch [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers contact [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers status [--out DIR]

  inputs    the subjects' frames, terrain plans and the game's map art, remembered in the run's state.json (the
            game's folders default to the Atlas' run's); --zones 1436,1446 --instances 36,209,3065
  paint     the levels in order (the far level takes a piece of the close one: a zone without one another zone's on
            its map, or its plan), what is missing only; --only zone-1436,...; --whole-close: the close level over
            the whole frame (a sample paints a zone's main place, an instance's middle)
  join      every subject's close squares joined again, cut along the line where neighbours differ least
  stitch    stitch-<level>-<continent>.jpg: the painted zones' middle and far levels each where it lies on its
            continent, cut by its outline (Navigation's zone masks), the zones' whole paintings under them
  contact   contact.jpg: a row per subject: its terrain plan, the game's map, close, middle and far
  status    its first line: "<made> of <all> paintings made"

It writes, in the run's folder (default assets/generated/map-layers, never committed), per subject <name>
(zone-<uimap>, instance-<map> and its floors instance-<map>-<floor>):
  <name>/ground.png, art.png          the terrain plan and the game's map art in the frame
  <name>/close/<i>-<j>.png, close.png the close squares, painted, and joined
  <name>/parchment.png                the middle level's image 3
  <name>/far-colours.png              the far level's image 1; far-height.png, far-water.png (a zone) or far-edges.png
                                      (an instance) its controls; far-walk.png the walking data (a zone); far-close.png
                                      its image 2
  <name>/middle-<try>.png, far-<try>.png and checks.json (each try's outline agreement and the vision model's answer)
  state.json                          the paths and subjects
Needs Python with Pillow and NumPy, and ComfyUI; the game's files as dev/tools/atlas, dev/tools/indoor-art and
the private baseline extract them (an instance's terrain in a folder of its own: wdt/, adt/, adt-obj0/, minimap/,
wmo/, wmo-minimap/, tiles.jsonl).
